  <section class="tg-section" id="routes">
    <h2>Getting to Tadoba, by region</h2>
    <p class="tg-lead">Tadoba sits almost exactly in the centre of India, and Nagpur, the city closest to it, is a domestic hub with a couple of useful international links. Here's how the journey usually works.</p>
    <div class="tg-table-wrap">
      <table class="tg-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Coming from</th><th>Typical routing</th><th>Travel time</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{routes.map((r) => <tr><td><strong>{r.from}</strong></td><td>{r.how}</td><td>{r.time}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
        <caption>Indicative only. Airline schedules change, and the Doha–Nagpur service runs on selected days of the week.</caption>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tg-section" id="step-by-step">
    <h2>Step by step: from your front door to the safari gate</h2>
    <ol class="tg-steps">
      <li><strong>Get your visa sorted.</strong> Apply for an Indian e-Tourist Visa online if your nationality is eligible. Do this weeks ahead, not days.</li>
      <li><strong>Fly into Delhi or Mumbai</strong> (or straight to Nagpur via Doha or Sharjah). Clear immigration and customs at your first Indian airport.</li>
      <li><strong>Take a domestic flight to Nagpur.</strong> About 1 hour 30–45 minutes from Delhi or Mumbai. Leave at least 3 hours between flights if you're changing terminals or airlines.</li>
      <li><strong>Drive to the reserve.</strong> Kolara gate is about 2–2.5 hours from Nagpur, Moharli about 3–3.5 hours. A pre-booked private car is the norm.</li>
      <li><strong>Rest, then safari.</strong> Arrive the day before your first drive, because gates open between 5:30 and 6:30 am.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="tg-section" id="visa">
    <h2>Visa, passport and safari documents</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Visa:</strong> most visitors need one. Many nationalities can use the Indian <a href="https://indianvisaonline.gov.in/evisa/" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">e-Visa</a>. Always use the official government portal, never a third-party site.</li>
      <li><strong>Passport at the gate:</strong> Tadoba's rules say foreign nationals must show their <strong>original passport</strong>. Photocopies and phone photos are refused, and the number must match the one on your safari permit exactly.</li>
      <li><strong>Permits in your name:</strong> safari permits are issued per guest. We need your passport details when booking.</li>
      <li><strong>Full-day safaris:</strong> foreign visitors pay {inr(fullDayFees[1].total)} per Gypsy for a full-day buffer safari (Indian visitors pay {inr(fullDayFees[0].total)}). Regular morning and evening drives use the standard Gypsy fees.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="tg-section" id="season">
    <h2>When to come</h2>
    <div class="tg-split">
      <div>
        <p><strong>March to June</strong> is peak tiger season. Water is scarce, animals gather at the few remaining pools, and sightings are at their best. It's also very hot: expect 40°C and above in May.</p>
        <p><strong>November to February</strong> is the comfortable choice: cool, misty mornings, warm afternoons and very good sightings. Pack a warm layer for the open Gypsy at dawn.</p>
        <p><strong>July to September</strong>, the core is closed for the monsoon. Only buffer drives run, weather permitting.</p>
      </div>
      <Image src={seasonImg} alt="Tiger standing in green forest" widths={[480, 800]} sizes="(max-width: 767px) 100vw, 360px" format="webp" quality={72} loading="lazy" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tg-section" id="combine">
    <h2>Combining Tadoba with more of India</h2>
    <div class="tg-split">
      <Image src={circuitImg} alt="Tiger walking through the forest at dusk" widths={[480, 800]} sizes="(max-width: 767px) 100vw, 360px" format="webp" quality={72} loading="lazy" />
      <div>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Tadoba + Pench:</strong> both are within easy reach of Nagpur. Two reserves, one domestic flight.</li>
          <li><strong>Tadoba + Kanha or Bandhavgarh:</strong> a classic central India tiger circuit, with a long but scenic road transfer.</li>
          <li><strong>Tadoba + the Golden Triangle:</strong> Delhi, Agra and Jaipur either side of a Nagpur flight.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>See our <a href="/tours/tigers-of-central-india/">14-day Tigers of Central India</a> trip, or ask us to tailor one.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tg-section" id="practical">
    <h2>Practical tips for international visitors</h2>
    <div class="tg-cards">
      <div class="tg-card"><div class="tg-card-body"><h3>Money</h3><p>Cards work at most lodges, but carry some rupees for tips and small purchases. ATMs are scarce near the gates.</p></div></div>
      <div class="tg-card"><div class="tg-card-body"><h3>Connectivity</h3><p>Mobile signal is patchy near the forest. Buy an Indian SIM or eSIM on arrival, and download offline maps.</p></div></div>
      <div class="tg-card"><div class="tg-card-body"><h3>Health</h3><p>Talk to a travel clinic about vaccines and malaria advice before you go. Carry sunscreen, repellent and any regular medicines.</p></div></div>
      <div class="tg-card"><div class="tg-card-body"><h3>Clothing</h3><p>Neutral colours for safaris, a warm layer for winter mornings, and modest clothing for towns and villages.</p></div></div>
    </div>
  </section>
</TadobaGuideLayout>
